          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            is the “correct” notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            being “naturally isomorphic” if they are the images of two functors that are naturally isomorphic. However, sometimes one encounters functors that are “pointwise isomorphic” but
            %em not
            naturally. There is not much to say
            %em mathematically
            about such “unnatural isomorphisms”, but some discussion of them can help the intuitive understanding of natural isomorphisms and their importance.
          %p
            On this page we will adhere to the convention that an “unnatural isomorphism” means an isomorphism that is not
            %em necessarily
            natural (but might be), similarly to how a “noncommutative
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            terms there are many more unnatural isomorphisms than natural ones, although in mathematical practice surprisingly many isomorphisms are indeed natural. Other possible terms with the same meaning are “pointwise isomorphic” or “objectwise isomorphic”.
          %p
            The term “natural” is used for traditional reasons, cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane45"} EilenbergMac Lane45
            = succeed "," do
              %em natural
            %em normal
            or
            %em regular
            tend to be rather non-generic or non-random. In fact,
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane45"} Eilenberg and MacLane
            comment (cf. p. 233, first paragraph in loc. cit) on the term, associating it with an intuition of
            = succeed "," do
              %em simultaneity
            = succeed "." do
              %em commutatitivity
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#between_functors Between functors
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G:C\to D
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %em unnatural isomorphism
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            consists of, for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c) \cong G(c)
          %p
            In other words, it is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unnatural+transformation"} unnatural transformation
            whose components are isomorphisms, or equivalently that is an isomorphism in the category of unnatural transformations.
          %p
            Note that if each component of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            is an isomorphism, then it is necessarily a natural isomorphism. (Depending on the definition of “natural isomorphism”, this could be a tautology; but the non-tautologous statement is that if each component of a natural transformation is an isomorphism then it is an isomorphism in the functor category. This statement, though non-tautologous, is easy to prove for 1-categories, but more difficult for higher categories.)
          %p
            More generally, an unnatural isomorphism (or transformation) might be natural with respect to only
            %em some
            morphisms in the domain, such as for instance the isomorphisms. Some people have proposed calling transformations that are natural only with respect to isomorphisms “canonical”.
          %h3#between_objects Between objects
          %p
            If one says that two
            %em objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are “unnaturally isomorphic”, it might mean that they are the images of two functors that are unnaturally isomorphic, but it might also mean only that there is no obvious way even to make them the images of two functors. For instance, this seems to be the meaning in Section 1.1 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Strickland1998"} Strickland
          %p
            Somewhat relatedly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+complex+numbers"} p-adic complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}_p
            are “unnaturally isomorphic”. More precisely, there is no way of making constructions such as “algebraic closure of a field” or “injective hull of an
            = succeed "-module\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AHRT"} here
          %h2#reasons_for_unnaturality Reasons for unnaturality
          %p Intuitively, there are several reasons that an isomorphism might fail to be natural.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Two functors of opposite variances (one covariant and one contravariant)
                %em cannot
                be naturally isomorphic in the usual sense. However, they can be
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dinatural+transformation"} dinaturally isomorphic
            %li
              %p In defining an isomorphism, it may be necessary to make certain arbitrary choices. If making these choices differently would yield a different isomorphism, then usually the isomorphism will not be natural.
          %h2#making_the_unnatural_natural Making the unnatural natural
          %p Unnatural isomorphisms are not very well-behaved mathematically, so if we have an unnatural isomorphism we may want to “make it natural”. There are several ways to do this:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Use a different definition that includes more structure, such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivators"} derivators
                instead of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triangulated_category#Are_there_better_axioms.3F"} Wikipedia discussion
                on axioms for triangulated categories.
            %li
              %p
                Relatedly, pass to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+categories"} higher-categorical
                context in which a non-commuting square can commute up to a higher transformation; see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformation"} pseudonatural transformation
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax natural transformation
            %li
              %p Cut down to a smaller class of morphisms in the domain category for which the naturality square does commute.
          %h2#natural_canonical_and_functorial Natural, canonical, and functorial
          %p
            The use of adjectives like
            = succeed "," do
              %em canonical
            = succeed "," do
              %em invariant
            = succeed "," do
              %em functorial
            %em natural
            is sometimes satirized. For example, as quoted in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MathOverflow2010"} MO2010
          %blockquote
            %p
              I can assure you, at any rate, that (…) my results are invariant, probably canonical, perhaps even functorial. (
              %em> Oeuvres
              , vol. 2, page 558)
          %p which may be subtly mocking.
          %p These three terms can be distinguished in the following way:
          %ul
            %li
              The definition of a single function or operation is “canonical” if there are “no arbitrary choices” involved in it, or more precisely if the end result is independent of any arbitrary choices that we had to make in defining it. In category theory, we generally consider something “canonical” if this independence is “up to unique isomorphism” in an appropriate sense, such as the definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
              limit functor on a category with limits.
            %li
              The definition of a function taking the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              of a category into the objects of another is
              %em functorial
              if we have also supplied a function on morphisms making it into a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %li
              The definition of a function taking the objects of one category into morphisms of another is
              %em natural
              if it forms a natural isomorphism between two functors (generally assumed to be given).
          %p In particular, the adjectives “functorial” and “natural” in general apply to different classes of things.
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-canonical+isomorphism"} non-canonical isomorphism
            for examples of
            %em natural
            isomorphisms that are not
            = succeed "." do
              %em canonical
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo :
                      %mi Vect
                      %mo →
                      %mi Aff
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: Vect\to Aff
                be the functor taking the underlying
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
                of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo :
                      %mi Aff
                      %mo →
                      %mi Vect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D:Aff\to Vect
                the functor constructing the vector space of displacements of an affine space. Then there is a
                %em natural
                isomorphism
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi U
                        %mo ≅
                        %msub
                          %mi Id
                          %mi Vect
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \circ U \cong Id_{Vect}
                %em unnatural
                isomorphism “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi D
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi Aff
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \circ D \cong Id_{Aff}
                ”. In particular, these functors do not form an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heaps"} heaps
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsors"} torsors
                of groups.
            %li
              %p
                As a particular example of the last phenomenon: there is a bijective correspondence between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linear orderings
                of a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Lin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lin(S)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
                over
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Perm
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Perm(S)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi Perm
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Lin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Lin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: Perm(S) \times Lin(S) \to Lin(S)
                such that for any
                %em choice
                of linear ordering
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Perm
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Lin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(-, L): Perm(S) \to Lin(S)
                is a bijection. But, there is no canonical choice of ordering
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} Species
                are functors for which unnatural isomorphisms are sometimes discussed. For example, the species of linear orders is unnaturally isomorphic to the species of permutations; for a related example, see the commentary under this MathOverflow answer
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#GesselTrimble2014"} MO2014
                %a{:href => "#Yorgey2014"} Yorgey2014
                explicitly uses the term: his definition 3.3.4 introduces an “equipotence between species (…) as an ”unnatural“ isomorphism between [the two species in question]”.
            %li
              %p
                The term
                %em unnatural isomorphism
                is often used in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#HiltonStammbach71"} Hilton-Stammbach, Theorem 4.3
                %em unnatural transformations
                also often occur in homological algebra.
            %li
              %p
                It is often said that while there is an isomorphism between the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                and the completion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi C
                        %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}_p
                (with respect to the canonical absolute value) of the algebraic closure of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+numbers"} p-adic numbers
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_p
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#AHR"} Adámek, Herrlich, Rosický, and Tholen
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                have only one object (or slightly more generally, one
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+class"} isomorphism class
                of objects), then any two functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G:C\to D
                are unnaturally isomorphic. For example, for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                are unnaturally isomorphic.
            %li
              %p
                See
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#MathOverflow2013"} MathOverflow2013
